# Quarterback / worker delegation — AUTOMATIC

**This rule is always on.** The main agent (quarterback) owns decomposition, delegation, merge, re-gate, and user delivery. Workers own implementation only.

## Roles

### Quarterback (main agent)

- Decompose the task and decide what to delegate vs. do inline.
- Delegate heavy work to Task subagents/workers.
- Merge worker output into the working tree.
- **Re-run both gate scripts on every changed file** before delivering to the user.
- Own the final response to the user.

### Workers (Task subagents)

- Implementation only — edit files, run gates on their own changes.
- Return a **file list** and **gate status** for every file they touched.
- **Never message the user** — no direct replies, no partial deliverables to the user.

## When to delegate vs. inline

| Delegate to workers | Do inline |
|---|---|
| 3+ files to change | Single small fix |
| Research + implementation split | One-file, low-risk edit |
| Long investigation or broad exploration | Quick localized change |

When delegating, give workers a scoped task, expected outputs (files + gate status), and clear boundaries.

## Re-gate before delivery (quarterback)

After merging worker changes, the quarterback **MUST** re-run both gate scripts on **every changed file** before including code in the user response:

```bash
python3 ~/.cursor/cursor_gate_fastest.py --file <path> --region us-west-2
python3 ~/.cursor/cursor_gate.py --file <path> --iterations 3
```

Fix failures and repeat until both return `"status": "PASS"` for each file. Worker gate passes do not substitute for quarterback re-gating.

## Forbidden

- ❌ Workers messaging the user (directly or via partial deliverables)
- ❌ Quarterback delivering code without re-running both gates on every changed file
- ❌ Quarterback assuming worker gate results are sufficient without re-gating
